@page "/how-it-works"
@implements IAsyncDisposable
@inject IJSRuntime JS

<PageTitle>StudyApp — How it works</PageTitle>

<div @ref="_pageRef">

    <h1 class="mb-1">How review and progress work</h1>
    <p class="text-muted mb-4">
        The pedagogy behind the schedule, and the exact math behind Mastery and Recall — no black boxes.
        One card's journey runs through every section below, so the numbers stay connected.
    </p>

    <nav class="how-toc mb-5">
        <a href="#pedagogy">Why spaced repetition</a>
        <a href="#algorithm">How review scheduling works</a>
        <a href="#practice">Practice vs review</a>
        <a href="#progress">How Mastery &amp; Recall are calculated</a>
        <a href="#future">Why this can change later</a>
    </nav>

    <section id="pedagogy" class="mb-5">
        <h2>Why review on a schedule instead of just re-reading?</h2>
        <p>
            Anything you learn starts fading the moment you stop looking at it. The psychologist Hermann
            Ebbinghaus mapped this over a century ago: memory drops fastest right after you learn something,
            then the drop-off flattens out. Re-reading or highlighting notes doesn't fight that curve — it just
            delays the moment you notice you've forgotten.
        </p>
        <p>Two ideas do the actual work here:</p>
        <ul>
            <li>
                <strong>Active recall.</strong> Trying to produce the answer yourself, before you see it,
                exercises the same retrieval you'll need in an exam or a conversation — which is why a card
                shows the question first and makes you grade your own honesty, instead of showing question and
                answer side by side.
            </li>
            <li>
                <strong>The spacing effect.</strong> Reviewing right as you're about to forget something
                strengthens the memory more than reviewing it while it's still fresh. And each time that
                works, the next gap before you'd forget again gets longer — so the reviews themselves get
                rarer even as the material gets more solid.
            </li>
        </ul>

        @((MarkupString)SpacingCurveSvg())
        <p class="text-muted small mb-4">
            One card, reviewed three times. Each review lands right around the point recall would have dropped
            to about 90% — not so early it's wasted, not so late the card was actually forgotten. Succeeding
            pushes the next gap out further: 1 day, then 6, then 15. The exact numbers behind this curve are
            the subject of the next two sections.
        </p>

        <div class="alert alert-light border mb-0">
            This app schedules every card this way automatically. You never decide when to study something
            next — you only grade how the last attempt went, and the schedule follows from that.
        </div>
    </section>

    <section id="algorithm" class="mb-5">
        <h2>The algorithm: SM-2</h2>
        <p>
            Scheduling is handled by <strong>SM-2</strong>, the algorithm behind Anki and most flashcard
            software since the 1980s. Every card carries a bit of state — how many days until it's due
            (<em>interval</em>), and a personal difficulty dial called the <em>ease factor</em>, which starts
            at <strong>2.5</strong> for every new card and drifts up or down based on how you grade it.
        </p>

        <h3 class="h5 mt-4">The four grades</h3>
        <div class="table-responsive">
            <table class="table table-sm align-middle">
                <thead>
                    <tr>
                        <th>Grade</th>
                        <th>You...</th>
                        <th>While still Learning</th>
                        <th>Once it's graduated to Review</th>
                    </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                    <tr>
                        <td><span class="badge text-bg-danger">Again</span></td>
                        <td>Got it wrong</td>
                        <td>Repeats right away, no penalty yet</td>
                        <td>Counts as a lapse; ease drops by 0.20; back to Learning, due immediately</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td><span class="badge text-bg-warning">Hard</span></td>
                        <td>Right, but it was a struggle</td>
                        <td>Repeats right away, no penalty</td>
                        <td>Interval barely grows (×1.2); ease drops by 0.15</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td><span class="badge text-bg-success">Good</span></td>
                        <td>Right, at a normal pace</td>
                        <td>Graduates: 1 day, then 6 days</td>
                        <td>Interval grows by the card's ease factor (starts at ×2.5)</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td><span class="badge text-bg-primary">Easy</span></td>
                        <td>Right, trivially</td>
                        <td>Graduates straight to 4 days</td>
                        <td>Interval grows extra fast (×ease×1.3); ease itself increases</td>
                    </tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>
        </div>
        <p class="text-muted small">
            Ease has a floor of 1.3× — a card can get slower to grow, but never grinds toward zero — and no
            interval is ever scheduled more than 365 days out.
        </p>

        <h3 class="h5 mt-4">Following one card</h3>
        <p>Same card as the diagram above, extended: what happens if you keep pressing Good.</p>
        <div class="table-responsive">
            <table class="table table-sm">
                <thead>
                    <tr>
                        <th>Review</th>
                        <th>You pressed</th>
                        <th>New interval</th>
                        <th>Bucket</th>
                        <th>Mastery right after</th>
                    </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                    <tr><td>1</td><td>Good</td><td>1 day</td><td>Young</td><td>5%</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>2</td><td>Good</td><td>6 days</td><td>Young</td><td>29%</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>3</td><td>Good</td><td>15 days</td><td>Young</td><td>71%</td></tr>
                    <tr class="table-success">
                        <td>4</td><td>Good</td><td>38 days</td><td>Mature 🎉</td><td>100% (capped)</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr><td>5</td><td>Good</td><td>95 days</td><td>Mature</td><td>100%</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>6</td><td>Good</td><td>238 days</td><td>Mature</td><td>100%</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>7</td><td>Good</td><td>365 days (capped)</td><td>Mature</td><td>100%</td></tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>
        </div>
        <p class="text-muted small">
            Four consecutive "Good"s and this card crosses the 21-day line and becomes Mature — the point
            Anki's convention treats as durably learned. Mastery (explained below) reads that same line.
        </p>

        <h3 class="h5 mt-4">Where a card sits</h3>
        <div class="flow-row">
            <div class="flow-step flow-unseen">
                <div class="flow-title">Unseen</div>
                <div class="flow-sub">Never reviewed</div>
            </div>
            <div class="flow-arrow">→</div>
            <div class="flow-step flow-learning">
                <div class="flow-title">Learning</div>
                <div class="flow-sub">Being drilled this session</div>
            </div>
            <div class="flow-arrow">→</div>
            <div class="flow-step flow-young">
                <div class="flow-title">Young</div>
                <div class="flow-sub">In Review, interval &lt; 21 days</div>
            </div>
            <div class="flow-arrow">→</div>
            <div class="flow-step flow-mature">
                <div class="flow-title">Mature</div>
                <div class="flow-sub">In Review, interval ≥ 21 days</div>
            </div>
        </div>
        <div class="flow-again">
            ↩ <strong>Again</strong> sends a card back to Learning from anywhere on this line — and once it's
            past Learning, it also costs a lapse. Eight lapses on one card marks it a <em>leech</em> (more on
            that below): something worth rewriting rather than keep re-losing time on.
        </div>
        <p class="text-muted small mt-3 mb-0">
            One more scheduling detail: the study day rolls over at 4am local time, not midnight — so a card
            reviewed at 11pm doesn't come due again the moment the clock ticks past 12, and a 1am session
            still counts as "tonight."
        </p>
    </section>

    <section id="practice" class="mb-5">
        <h2>Practice vs review — why drilling a topic doesn't move its dates</h2>
        <p>
            The schedule above answers "what should I see today". But sometimes you want to work on one
            thing — a chapter, a topic, a stage of the learning path — and most of it won't be due. That's
            what <strong>⟳ Practise</strong> is for, on the
            <a href="study">Practise something specific</a> page.
        </p>
        <p>
            A card graded <em>before</em> its due date is <strong>practice</strong>, and practice
            deliberately changes nothing about its schedule: same due date, same interval, same
            repetition count, and a wrong answer costs no lapse. It's decided per card, so a session
            can hold a mix — anything genuinely due is an ordinary review that advances normally.
        </p>
        <div class="alert alert-secondary small">
            <strong>Why not just grade it normally?</strong> Because an early success isn't evidence the
            interval was too short — you only recalled it because you asked to see it. Letting that stretch
            the interval would mean drilling a topic before an exam quietly pushed all its cards further
            away, and inflated the Mastery figure built from those intervals. The number would rise
            because you practised, not because you knew more.
        </div>
        <p class="mb-0">
            Practice reviews <em>are</em> recorded — the work happened — but they're left out of the
            30-day retention figure, which asks a narrower question: when the scheduler predicted you were
            about to forget, were you right? A review you requested early was never that test.
        </p>
    </section>

    <section id="progress" class="mb-5">
        <h2>How Mastery and Recall are calculated</h2>
        <p>
            Progress is reported as <strong>two independent numbers</strong>, on purpose, instead of one
            score. A single percentage would either hide a wall of overdue cards behind a good-looking
            number, or fall every night while you sleep, whether or not you did anything wrong. Splitting it
            avoids both:
        </p>
        <ul>
            <li><strong>Mastery</strong> — how durably learned a card is. Only moves when you study.</li>
            <li><strong>Recall now</strong> — the odds you'd get it right this instant. Decays with elapsed
                time by design, because forgetting is real and hiding it would make the number useless for
                deciding what to study.</li>
        </ul>

        <h3 class="h5 mt-4">Mastery — how durably learned is this card?</h3>
        <p>It's just the card's interval, scaled against the same 21-day line from the table above, and capped at 1:</p>
        <div class="formula-box">
            \[
            \text{Mastery} = \operatorname{clamp}\!\left(\dfrac{\text{interval (days)}}{21},\ 0,\ 1\right)
            \]
        </div>
        <p>
            New and Learning cards score exactly 0 — nothing has been proven durable yet. Cards keep the
            interval growing well past 38 days in the table above (95, then 238, then the 365-day cap), but
            Mastery stops climbing at 21: hitting 100% means "crossed the durable-learning line," not "this
            card is finished forever."
        </p>

        <h3 class="h5 mt-4">Recall now — what are your odds this instant?</h3>
        <p>
            This one follows the forgetting curve directly, anchored at the same 90% every scheduler — SM-2
            today, FSRS later — aims for right when a card comes due:
        </p>
        <div class="formula-box">
            \[
            \text{RecallNow} = 0.9^{\,\text{days since last review}\ /\ \text{interval (days)}}
            \]
        </div>
        <p>
            Because of that anchor, a card exactly on schedule always reads ~90%, no matter how long its
            interval has grown to. Reviewed early, it reads higher; left late, it reads lower. Zooming in on
            review 3 from the table above — a 15-day interval — shows the shape:
        </p>

        @((MarkupString)RecallZoomSvg())
        <p class="text-muted small mb-4">
            Show up right after reviewing and it's ~100%. Show up exactly on schedule (day 15) and it's the
            anchor, 90%. Let it sit twice as long (day 30, two weeks overdue) and it's dropped to 81% —
            the same curve cut short in the very first diagram, continued past the point that card was
            actually reviewed.
        </p>

        <h3 class="h5 mt-4">The buckets</h3>
        <div class="table-responsive">
            <table class="table table-sm">
                <thead><tr><th>Bucket</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead>
                <tbody>
                    <tr><td><span class="hiw-swatch hiw-swatch-unseen"></span> Unseen</td><td>Never reviewed (State = New)</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><span class="hiw-swatch hiw-swatch-learning"></span> Learning</td><td>Being drilled this session, not yet graduated</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><span class="hiw-swatch hiw-swatch-young"></span> Young</td><td>Graduated, interval under 21 days</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><span class="hiw-swatch hiw-swatch-mature"></span> Mature</td><td>Graduated, interval 21 days or more — durably learned</td></tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>
        </div>

        <h3 class="h5 mt-4">The rest of the progress card</h3>
        <dl class="row">
            <dt class="col-sm-3">Overdue</dt>
            <dd class="col-sm-9">
                A card is overdue once its due date has passed and you haven't reviewed it yet. Remember the
                4am rollover from the scheduling section — "today" runs later than midnight.
            </dd>

            <dt class="col-sm-3">Leeches</dt>
            <dd class="col-sm-9">
                A card with 8 or more lapses — Anki's own threshold. Averaging it into Mastery would bury the
                one thing worth acting on, so it gets listed by name instead: usually the fix is rewriting or
                splitting the card, not reviewing it harder.
            </dd>

            <dt class="col-sm-3">30-day retention</dt>
            <dd class="col-sm-9">
                The share of graded reviews in the last 30 days that weren't "Again," straight from your
                review history. This is the honesty check on Mastery: Mastery is an average across every card
                in a set, so it can keep drifting upward from your successes even while a real chunk of your
                daily reviews are failing. Retention answers a narrower, more direct question — of the
                reviews you actually did, how many did you get right? — and it's the only one of these numbers
                that comes from what actually happened rather than from where a card currently sits.
            </dd>

            <dt class="col-sm-3">Readiness forecast</dt>
            <dd class="col-sm-9">
                Shown against an assignment's due date on the home page: of the cards in that course, how many
                are already scheduled to stay durable through that date without another review, versus how
                many still need at least one more successful review before then. It's a projection under
                "if you keep up your reviews" — never a promise.
            </dd>
        </dl>
    </section>

    <section id="future" class="mb-4">
        <h2>Why this can change later</h2>
        <p>
            SM-2 is deliberately kept behind a seam (<code>IScheduler</code>) so it can be swapped for a
            newer algorithm — most likely <strong>FSRS</strong>, a more recent model that fits its intervals
            to each person's actual memory rather than one fixed ease dial — without disturbing anything
            downstream.
        </p>
        <p class="mb-0">
            That's why Mastery and Recall are built only from a card's interval, lapse count, state, and
            review history — never from the ease factor above, which is specific to SM-2 and won't exist once
            it's replaced. The numbers on your deck and course pages will keep meaning the same thing before
            and after that swap.
        </p>
    </section>

</div>

@code {
    private ElementReference _pageRef;
    private IJSObjectReference? _mathJs;
    private bool _mathRendered;

    protected override async Task OnAfterRenderAsync(bool firstRender)
    {
        if (_mathRendered)
            return;
        try
        {
            _mathJs ??= await JS.InvokeAsync<IJSObjectReference>("import", "/js/mathRender.js");
            await _mathJs.InvokeVoidAsync("renderMath", _pageRef);
            _mathRendered = true;
        }
        catch (Exception ex) when (ex is JSDisconnectedException or JSException)
        {
            // Retried on the next render; nothing to clean up.
        }
    }

    public async ValueTask DisposeAsync()
    {
        try
        {
            if (_mathJs is not null)
                await _mathJs.DisposeAsync();
        }
        catch (JSDisconnectedException)
        {
            // Circuit already gone; nothing to clean up client-side.
        }
    }

    /// <summary>
    /// The three-review overview diagram in the pedagogy section. Uses the exact
    /// RecallNow formula (0.9^fraction-of-interval-elapsed) for each segment, so the curve
    /// is a real illustration of the math, not an artist's impression of one.
    /// </summary>
    private static string SpacingCurveSvg()
    {
        double[] intervals = [1, 6, 15];
        const int width = 620, height = 200, padL = 42, padR = 16, padT = 16, padB = 30;
        const double pctMin = 85, pctMax = 100;
        var totalDays = intervals.Sum();
        var plotW = width - padL - padR;
        var plotH = height - padT - padB;

        double X(double day) => padL + day / totalDays * plotW;
        double Y(double pct) => padT + (pctMax - pct) / (pctMax - pctMin) * plotH;

        var curves = new List<string>();
        var markers = new List<string> { $"<circle cx='{X(0):0.#}' cy='{Y(100):0.#}' r='4' class='hiw-dot hiw-dot-review' />" };
        var dayCursor = 0.0;

        foreach (var interval in intervals)
        {
            var pts = new List<string>();
            for (var i = 0; i <= 40; i++)
            {
                var frac = i / 40.0;
                var day = dayCursor + frac * interval;
                var pct = 100 * Math.Pow(0.9, frac);
                pts.Add($"{X(day):0.##},{Y(pct):0.##}");
            }
            curves.Add($"<polyline points='{string.Join(' ', pts)}' class='hiw-curve' />");

            var reviewDay = dayCursor + interval;
            curves.Add($"<line x1='{X(reviewDay):0.#}' y1='{Y(90):0.#}' x2='{X(reviewDay):0.#}' y2='{Y(100):0.#}' class='hiw-boost' />");
            markers.Add($"<circle cx='{X(reviewDay):0.#}' cy='{Y(90):0.#}' r='4' class='hiw-dot' />");
            markers.Add($"<circle cx='{X(reviewDay):0.#}' cy='{Y(100):0.#}' r='4' class='hiw-dot hiw-dot-review' />");

            dayCursor = reviewDay;
        }

        var guideY = Y(90);
        var guide = $"<line x1='{padL}' y1='{guideY:0.#}' x2='{width - padR}' y2='{guideY:0.#}' class='hiw-guide' />";
        var guideLabel = $"<text x='{width - padR}' y='{guideY - 6:0.#}' text-anchor='end' class='hiw-label'>~90% — right when each review is due</text>";

        var axisLabels = new[]
        {
            $"<text x='{padL}' y='{height - 6}' class='hiw-label'>Day 0</text>",
            $"<text x='{X(1):0.#}' y='{height - 6}' text-anchor='middle' class='hiw-label'>1</text>",
            $"<text x='{X(7):0.#}' y='{height - 6}' text-anchor='middle' class='hiw-label'>7</text>",
            $"<text x='{X(22):0.#}' y='{height - 6}' text-anchor='end' class='hiw-label'>22</text>",
        };

        return $"""
            <svg viewBox="0 0 {width} {height}" width="100%" height="{height}" class="hiw-svg" role="img"
                 aria-label="Recall percentage over three successive reviews, dropping toward 90% then resetting, with the gap between reviews growing each time.">
                {guide}
                {guideLabel}
                {string.Join("\n", curves)}
                {string.Join("\n", markers)}
                {string.Join("\n", axisLabels)}
            </svg>
            """;
    }

    /// <summary>Zooms into the single 15-day interval from the worked table, for the RecallNow section.</summary>
    private static string RecallZoomSvg()
    {
        const double interval = 15;
        const int width = 560, height = 190, padL = 20, padR = 20, padT = 16, padB = 36;
        const double pctMin = 75, pctMax = 100, maxElapsed = 30;
        var plotW = width - padL - padR;
        var plotH = height - padT - padB;

        double X(double elapsed) => padL + elapsed / maxElapsed * plotW;
        double Y(double pct) => padT + (pctMax - pct) / (pctMax - pctMin) * plotH;

        var pts = new List<string>();
        for (var i = 0; i <= 60; i++)
        {
            var elapsed = i / 60.0 * maxElapsed;
            var pct = 100 * Math.Pow(0.9, elapsed / interval);
            pts.Add($"{X(elapsed):0.##},{Y(pct):0.##}");
        }
        var curve = $"<polyline points='{string.Join(' ', pts)}' class='hiw-curve' />";

        var guideY = Y(90);
        var guide = $"<line x1='{padL}' y1='{guideY:0.#}' x2='{width - padR}' y2='{guideY:0.#}' class='hiw-guide' />";
        var guideLabel = $"<text x='{padL}' y='{guideY - 6:0.#}' class='hiw-label'>target: 90%</text>";

        (double elapsed, double pct, string label, string anchor)[] marks =
        [
            (0, 100, "just reviewed", "start"),
            (15, 90, "on schedule — day 15", "middle"),
            (30, 81, "2× late — day 30", "end"),
        ];

        var markers = new List<string>();
        foreach (var (elapsed, pct, label, anchor) in marks)
        {
            markers.Add($"<circle cx='{X(elapsed):0.#}' cy='{Y(pct):0.#}' r='4' class='hiw-dot hiw-dot-review' />");
            markers.Add($"<text x='{X(elapsed):0.#}' y='{Y(pct) - 10:0.#}' text-anchor='{anchor}' class='hiw-label'>{pct:0}%</text>");
            markers.Add($"<text x='{X(elapsed):0.#}' y='{height - 6}' text-anchor='{anchor}' class='hiw-label hiw-label-sub'>{label}</text>");
        }

        return $"""
            <svg viewBox="0 0 {width} {height}" width="100%" height="{height}" class="hiw-svg" role="img"
                 aria-label="Recall probability decaying from 100% to 81% over the 30 days following a review, crossing 90% at day 15.">
                {guide}
                {guideLabel}
                {curve}
                {string.Join("\n", markers)}
            </svg>
            """;
    }
}
